\title{Amortized Model-Delta Compression:\\ A Min-Plus Theory of Shared, Adaptable Decompressors}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{16 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A fixed, shared, pretrained decompressor combined with an arithmetic-coded residual is an
extremely strong lossless compressor for structured data such as natural-language text.
The scientific difficulty is that the decompressor is not free when it must be
\emph{steered}: the domain-adaptation patch --- a low-rank adapter, a sparse weight
difference, a codebook, a dictionary --- is itself a transmitted bitstring and must be
charged to the message. This paper develops a complete and sharp theory of that trade-off
in a deliberately austere model: a finite set $M$ of decoder states, a \emph{model-delta
cost} $\delta : M \times M \to \N$ giving the bits needed to move the shared decoder
between states, and per-message \emph{residual costs} $c : M \to \N$. The protocol optimum
is the minimum, over all schedules of decoder states, of the total transmitted bits.

We prove: (i) Bellman optimality for the min-plus dynamic program; (ii) a sharp
amortization law --- for a coherent stream of $n$ messages the optimum is exactly
$nr + \min(D,n)$ bits, so the break-even point is exactly at $n = D$ and the amortized rate
tends to $r$; (iii) an information-theoretic floor by pigeonhole, so the amortized protocol
lies within $D$ bits of optimal uniformly in $n$; (iv) a constructive losslessness gate;
(v) a tropical bridge identifying the dynamic program with powers of a min-plus matrix;
(vi) the coherence-length law --- for $B$ blocks of $L$ messages with alternating domain the
optimum is exactly $BLr + \lfloor B/2\rfloor\min(2D,L) + (B \bmod 2)\min(D,L)$, so the
amortized excess is $\min(2D,L)/(2L)$ bits per message; and (vii) a logarithmic warm-up
bound for a decompressor steerable to $K$ domains.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} min-plus semiring, tropical algebra, lossless compression,
amortized analysis, model delta, dynamic programming, coherence length, pigeonhole bound,
minimum description length.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The decompressor is a program}

Every lossless compressor is a pair: a \emph{program} (the decompressor) and \emph{data}
(the compressed bitstring). Classical compressors keep the program tiny and fixed and pour
all their effort into the data. Modern neural compressors invert the ratio: a pretrained
sequence model, used as a conditional probability oracle for an arithmetic coder, achieves
residual rates on text far below classical baselines, at the price of a program measured in
gigabytes.

This inversion is legitimate provided one is honest about accounting. Two regimes are
commonly conflated. Under a \emph{fixed shared decompressor}, sender and receiver agree at
deployment time on a universal artifact; it is negotiated once, never transmitted, and
identical for all users, so the compressed size is the residual stream alone. Under a
\emph{steered decompressor}, the sender improves the shared artifact for the domain at hand
by transmitting a patch; the patch \emph{is} part of the message and must be charged. The
first regime is uncontroversial but static; the second is where the engineering interest
lies and where the theory has been thin. This paper supplies the theory for the second
regime, sharply enough to yield exact break-even points and exact amortized rates.

\subsection{Contributions}

We isolate the problem as a scheduling problem over decoder states and solve it exactly in
several regimes. The technical backbone is that both operations involved --- accumulating
cost along a schedule and minimizing over schedules --- are the two operations of the
min-plus (tropical) semiring, so the theory is genuinely tropical rather than merely
analogous to it. Section~\ref{sec:model} fixes the model and proves Bellman optimality;
Section~\ref{sec:sharp} proves the sharp amortization law; Section~\ref{sec:floor} the
counting floor and the lossless construction; Section~\ref{sec:tropical} the tropical
bridge; Section~\ref{sec:coherence} the coherence-length law; Section~\ref{sec:patches} the
logarithmic warm-up; Section~\ref{sec:structure} structural laws; and
Sections~\ref{sec:algorithms}--\ref{sec:future} algorithms, applications and open problems.

\subsection{Relation to classical ideas}

The trade-off ``model bits plus data bits'' is the minimum description length principle,
and the ultimate version of the question is Kolmogorov complexity, uncomputable in general.
The contribution here is orthogonal to both: we take the residual rates as given --- they
are what a real coder measures --- and ask for the \emph{optimal dynamics} of model changes
over a stream. That question has a clean, computable and sharp answer, and its answer is a
shortest path.

\section{The model and Bellman optimality}\label{sec:model}

Throughout, $M$ is a finite nonempty set of \emph{decoder states}: any configuration the
shared decompressor can be placed in, i.e.\ the pretrained model as shipped, or that model
plus a specific patch. All costs are nonnegative integers of bits.

\begin{definition}[Model-delta cost]
A \emph{model-delta cost} is $\delta : M \times M \to \N$, where $\delta(m,m')$ is the
number of bits transmitted to move the shared decoder from state $m$ to state $m'$. It is
\emph{reflexive-free} if $\delta(m,m) = 0$ for all $m$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Residual cost, stream]
A \emph{residual cost} is $c : M \to \N$, where $c(m)$ is the number of bits needed to code
one message with the decoder in state $m$. A \emph{stream} is a finite list
$\mathbf{c} = (c_1,\dots,c_n)$ of residual costs.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Schedule and its cost]
Given a start state $p$ and a stream of length $n$, a \emph{schedule} is
$\mathbf{m} = (m_1,\dots,m_n) \in M^n$, and its cost is
\[
\sched(p,\mathbf{c},\mathbf{m}) = \sum_{i=1}^{n}\bigl(\delta(m_{i-1},m_i) + c_i(m_i)\bigr),
\qquad m_0 := p .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Protocol optimum]\label{def:opt}
The \emph{protocol optimum} $V_\delta$ is defined by
\[
V_\delta(p,()) = 0,\qquad
V_\delta(p, c :: \mathbf{c}) = \min_{m \in M}\bigl(\delta(p,m) + c(m) + V_\delta(m,\mathbf{c})\bigr).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Bellman optimality]\label{thm:bellman}
For every start state $p$ and every stream $\mathbf{c}$ of length $n$,
\[
V_\delta(p,\mathbf{c}) = \min\{\sched(p,\mathbf{c},\mathbf{m}) : \mathbf{m}\in M^n\},
\]
and the minimum is attained by an explicitly constructible schedule.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Two inductions on the stream. For the lower bound, unfold one step: the minimum in
Definition~\ref{def:opt} is at most the term indexed by $m_1$, and the inductive hypothesis
bounds the continuation by the cost of the schedule tail. For attainment, finiteness and
nonemptiness of $M$ let us choose a minimizing $m_1$ at each step; recursing produces a
schedule of cost exactly $V_\delta(p,\mathbf{c})$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:bellman} licenses every subsequent computation: bounds on $V_\delta$ are
bounds on \emph{every} protocol, not merely on the strategies a designer might invent.

\begin{proposition}[Pay the delta once]\label{prop:stay}
If $\delta$ is reflexive-free, then for every target state $m$, every stream and every
start $p$,
\[
V_\delta(p,\mathbf{c}) \le \delta(p,m) + \sum_{i=1}^{n} c_i(m).
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on the stream, choosing $m$ at each step. The first step pays $\delta(p,m)$; every
later step pays $\delta(m,m) = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The residual rate is a floor]\label{prop:floor}
If $r \le c_i(m)$ for all $i$ and all $m$, then $n r \le V_\delta(p,\mathbf{c})$ for every
$p$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct: every branch of the minimum pays $c_1(m) \ge r$ plus a continuation of at least
$(n-1)r$, hence is at least $nr$; so is the minimum.
\end{proof}

\section{The sharp amortization law}\label{sec:sharp}

We specialize to a \emph{coherent stream} of $n$ statistically identical messages,
$c_1 = \dots = c_n = c$, written $c^{\,n}$. Fix $r, D \in \N$ and assume:

\begin{itemize}
\item[(H1)] \textbf{Rate floor.} $r \le c(m)$ for all $m$. Call $m$ \emph{specialized} if
$c(m) = r$.
\item[(H2)] \textbf{Delta is unavoidable.} If $m'$ is specialized and $m$ is not, then
$D \le \delta(m,m')$.
\item[(H3)] \textbf{Delta is achievable.} There is a specialized $g$ with
$\delta(p,g) \le D$.
\item[(H4)] \textbf{Generic start.} $c(p) = r+1$.
\item[(H5)] \textbf{Staying is free.} $\delta(m,m) = 0$ for all $m$.
\end{itemize}

Hypothesis (H4) normalizes the per-message advantage of specialization to one bit; this is
a choice of unit, not a loss of generality.

\begin{proposition}[Amortized upper bound]\label{prop:upper}
Under (H3), (H5): $V_\delta(p, c^{\,n}) \le D + nr$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Proposition~\ref{prop:stay} with target $g$, using $c(g) = r$ and $\delta(p,g)\le D$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Delta lower bound]\label{thm:lower}
Assume (H1), (H2). For every $n$ and every non-specialized start $p$,
\[
nr + \min(D,n) \le V_\delta(p, c^{\,n}).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $n$; the case $n=0$ is trivial. For $n+1$ messages, bound every branch of the
minimum over the state $m$ chosen for the first message. If $m$ is specialized, then
$\delta(p,m)\ge D$ by (H2) and the tail costs at least $nr$ by
Proposition~\ref{prop:floor}, giving $\ge (n+1)r + D$. If $m$ is not specialized, then
$c(m)\ge r+1$ by (H1) and integrality, and the inductive hypothesis applies at $m$, giving
$\ge (n+1)r + 1 + \min(D,n)$. Both dominate $(n+1)r + \min(D,n+1)$.
\end{proof}

The dichotomy in the proof is the mathematical content of the slogan: either you pay the
delta once, or you pay one extra bit per message; the lower bound records that the cheaper
option is $\min(D,n)$.

\begin{theorem}[Sharp amortization law]\label{thm:sharp}
Under (H1)--(H5), for every $n$,
\[
V_\delta(p, c^{\,n}) \;=\; n r + \min(D, n).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The lower bound is Theorem~\ref{thm:lower}, applicable since $c(p) = r+1 \ne r$. For the
upper bound combine Proposition~\ref{prop:upper} ($\le D + nr$) with
Proposition~\ref{prop:stay} applied to the target $p$ itself ($\le n(r+1) = nr + n$).
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact break-even]\label{cor:breakeven}
$V_\delta(p, c^{\,n}) < n(r+1)$ if and only if $D < n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Short streams gain nothing]\label{cor:short}
If $n \le D$ then $V_\delta(p, c^{\,n}) = n(r+1)$ exactly, and the specialized state is
never entered.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Asymptotic freeness of the model delta]\label{thm:asym}
Under (H1), (H3), (H5), $\displaystyle \lim_{n\to\infty} V_\delta(p, c^{\,n})/n = r$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Squeeze between $r$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:floor}) and $r + D/n$
(Proposition~\ref{prop:upper}); the latter tends to $r$ for any fixed $D$, however large.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Economies of scale]
In the sharp regime $V(n) + V(n+2) \le 2V(n+1)$: the marginal cost of one more message is
non-increasing.
\end{proposition}

\subsection*{Non-vacuity: an explicit two-state witness}

Take $M = \{\gen, \spec\}$ with $c(\spec) = r$, $c(\gen) = r+1$, and $\delta(m,m') = D$ if
$(m,m') = (\gen,\spec)$ and $0$ otherwise. All five hypotheses hold, hence
$V_\delta(\gen, c^{\,n}) = nr + \min(D,n)$ exactly, with the break-even characterization of
Corollary~\ref{cor:breakeven}.

\section{The information-theoretic floor and the losslessness gate}\label{sec:floor}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:count}
There are exactly $2^{t+1}-1$ bitstrings of length at most $t$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction: strings of length $\le t+1$ are the empty string together with the two disjoint
injective images of the strings of length $\le t$ under prepending a bit, so
$N_{t+1}+1 = 2(N_t+1)$ with $N_0+1 = 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Compressible sources are exponentially rare]\label{thm:rare}
For any injective $\enc$ from a finite set of sources into bitstrings, the number of
sources with $|\enc(a)| \le t$ is at most $2^{t+1}-1$.
\end{theorem}

Injectivity is exactly losslessness. Note that nothing whatever is assumed about the
decompressor: it may be a table, a context mixer, or sixteen gigabytes of transformer
weights.

\begin{corollary}[Pigeonhole bound]\label{cor:pigeon}
With at least $2^{t+1}$ distinct sources, some source is transmitted in more than $t$ bits.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Streaming counting floor]\label{thm:streamfloor}
Let $s \ge 1$ and let $\enc$ be injective on streams of $n$ messages from an alphabet of
$2^s$ symbols. Then some stream $x$ satisfies $|\enc(x)| \ge ns$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
There are $2^{ns}$ streams; apply Corollary~\ref{cor:pigeon} with $t = ns-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Shared codec]
A \emph{shared codec} at rate $s$ over a message type $X$ is a pair
$\enc : X \to \{0,1\}^s$, $\dec : \{0,1\}^s \to X$. It is \emph{lossless on} $S \subseteq X$
if $\dec(\enc(x)) = x$ for all $x \in S$. Streams are coded message by message.
\end{definition}

The decoder is the shared decompressor fixed at deploy time; selecting \emph{which} codec is
in force is what the transmitted model delta buys.

\begin{theorem}[Existence of a domain-adapted codec]
If $|S| \le 2^s$ then some shared codec at rate $s$ is lossless on $S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$|S|\le 2^s$ gives an injection $e$ of $S$ into the codeword set; encode by $e$ and decode
by the unique preimage where one exists, arbitrarily elsewhere.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Losslessness gate]
If a codec is lossless on $S$ then it reconstructs every stream drawn from $S$ exactly,
message for message.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction along the list: apply the per-message round-trip to the head and the inductive
hypothesis to the tail.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Amortized protocol with full accounting]\label{thm:protocol}
Let $|S| \le 2^s$ and let $D$ be the length of the patch selecting the adapted codec. Then
some codec decodes every $S$-stream exactly and has total bit budget
$\mathrm{bits}(n) = D + ns$, satisfying $\mathrm{bits}(n) < n(s+1) \iff D < n$ and
$ns \le \mathrm{bits}(n)$.
\end{theorem}

Combining Theorems~\ref{thm:streamfloor} and \ref{thm:protocol} gives the sandwich
$ns \le \mathrm{bits}(n) = D + ns$: the protocol is within $D$ bits of the
information-theoretic optimum \emph{uniformly in $n$}, with relative gap
$D/(ns) \to 0$. This discharges the programme's falsifiability gate --- lossless decoding,
decoder fixed at deploy time, delta charged to the message, strict win past break-even.

\section{The tropical bridge}\label{sec:tropical}

The \emph{min-plus (tropical) semiring} is $(\N\cup\{\infty\},\oplus,\otimes)$ with
$a\oplus b = \min(a,b)$ (identity $\infty$) and $a\otimes b = a+b$ (identity $0$). Matrix
multiplication reads $(AB)_{ij} = \min_k (A_{ik}+B_{kj})$: shortest-path composition.

\begin{definition}[Cost matrix]
For $\delta$ and a residual cost $c$ define $A_{ij} = \delta(i,j) + c(j)$, the bits to move
the decoder from state $i$ to state $j$ and then code one message there.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[One DP step is one tropical linear step]
$V_\delta(p, c::\mathbf{c}) = \bigoplus_{m\in M}\bigl(\delta(p,m)\otimes c(m)\otimes
V_\delta(m,\mathbf{c})\bigr)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[The optimum is a tropical matrix power]\label{thm:power}
For a coherent stream of $n$ identical messages,
\[
\bigl(A^{\otimes n}\otimes \mathbf{1}\bigr)_p \;=\; V_\delta(p, c^{\,n}),
\]
where $\mathbf{1}$ is the tropical all-ones vector (all entries $0$).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $n$: the base case is $A^{\otimes 0} = I$ and $V_\delta(p,()) = 0$; the step
unfolds one min-plus matrix--vector product and matches it term by term against the
recursion.
\end{proof}

Two payoffs. First, in the two-state witness Theorems~\ref{thm:power} and \ref{thm:sharp}
combine to give the closed form
\[
\bigl(A^{\otimes n}\otimes \mathbf{1}\bigr)_{\gen} = nr + \min(D,n),
\]
a piecewise-linear function of $n$ with a single kink at $n=D$: the break-even point of the
protocol is literally a tropical corner. Second, since the amortized bits per message is
$(A^{\otimes n}\otimes\mathbf{1})_p/n$, the long-run rate is the growth rate of tropical
matrix powers, which for min-plus matrices is the \emph{minimum cycle mean} --- the
tropical analogue of the spectral radius. Hence: the asymptotic cost per message of any
streaming adaptation protocol should be the cheapest average weight of a cycle in the
decoder-state graph, with the optimal protocol eventually periodic along that cycle. Two
instances are proved below; the general statement is Conjecture~\ref{conj:c1}.

\section{Coherence length, not stream length}\label{sec:coherence}

Fix two domains $d\in\{0,1\}$ and two specialized states likewise indexed, with
$c_d(m) = r$ if $m=d$ and $r+1$ otherwise. Swapping states costs $D\ge 1$; staying is free.

\begin{theorem}[Exact optimum for the alternating stream]\label{thm:alt}
For $D \ge 1$, the stream that flips domain at every message satisfies
\[
V(p,\mathrm{alt}_d(n)) = nr + \begin{cases}\lfloor n/2\rfloor & p = d,\\
\lceil n/2\rceil & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
\]
In particular the value is independent of $D$: the optimal protocol never switches.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $n$ carrying both starting states. Over a two-element state set the DP minimum
is a binary minimum, so the recursion becomes a pair of coupled integer identities resolved
by case analysis; the key inequality is that a switch costs at least one bit and buys at
most one bit before the domain flips back.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The excess over the floor $nr$ is $\lceil n/2\rceil$, growing linearly, in contrast with
the bounded excess $\min(D,n)\le D$ of the coherent regime; the amortized rate is
$r + \tfrac12$; and for $n \ge 2D+2$ the coherent stream is strictly cheaper than the
alternating stream of the same length.
\end{corollary}

Stream length is therefore not the resource that amortizes the delta. Let the stream now
consist of $B$ blocks of $L$ consecutive messages, the domain alternating from block to
block; $L$ is the \emph{coherence length}.

\begin{lemma}[Block absorption]\label{lem:absorb}
For a block of $k$ messages of domain $d$ followed by a remainder $R$,
\begin{align*}
V(d, d^{\,k}R) &= kr + \min\bigl(V(d,R),\; D + V(\bar d, R)\bigr),\\
V(\bar d, d^{\,k}R) &= kr + \min\bigl(k + V(\bar d, R),\; D + V(d,R),\; 2D + V(\bar d, R)\bigr).
\end{align*}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $k$ carrying both starting states. The three terms are the three sensible
policies for a wrongly-positioned decoder: never switch (eat $k$ surplus bits), switch in
and stay ($D$), switch in and back out ($2D$). The triangle inequality for the swap cost
guarantees no further policy competes.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Block excess]
$E(0)=0$, $E(1) = \min(D,L)$, $E(B+2) = E(B) + \min(2D,L)$; and $G(0)=0$, $G(B+1)=E(B)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact optimum for block-alternating streams]\label{thm:block}
$V(\bar d, \mathrm{blocks}_d(B,L)) = BLr + E(B)$ and
$V(d,\mathrm{blocks}_d(B,L)) = BLr + G(B)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $B$, absorbing the leading block with Lemma~\ref{lem:absorb} and using the
recursion $E(B+1) = \min\bigl(L+G(B),\, D+E(B),\, 2D+G(B)\bigr)$, the three-policy
competition transported to block granularity; the monotonicity facts $E(B+1)\le D+E(B)$ and
$E(B)+\min(2D,L)\le D+E(B+1)$ keep the branches consistent.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Closed form and bounds]\label{thm:closed}
\[
E(B) = \Bigl\lfloor \tfrac{B}{2}\Bigr\rfloor \min(2D,L) + (B \bmod 2)\min(D,L),
\]
and $B\min(2D,L) \le 2E(B) \le B\min(2D,L) + 2\min(D,L)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Coherence-length law]\label{thm:coh}
For $L \ge 1$,
\[
\lim_{B\to\infty} \frac{V(\bar d, \mathrm{blocks}_d(B,L))}{BL} \;=\; r + \frac{\min(2D,L)}{2L}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Divide the closed form of Theorem~\ref{thm:closed} by $BL$ and squeeze with the two-sided
bounds; the leftover $\min(D,L)/(BL)$ vanishes.
\end{proof}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Regime & Excess per message & Interpretation \\
\midrule
$L \ge 2D$ & $D/L$ & delta amortizes against the block; overhead $\to 0$ \\
$L < 2D$ & $1/2$ & independent of $D$: patch never worth sending \\
$L = 1$ & $1/2$ & the alternating stream \\
$L \to \infty$ & $0$ & the coherent stream \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The design consequence is unambiguous: \textbf{the model delta amortizes against the
coherence length of the stream, not against its length}. Sorting a stream from coherence
length $L_1$ to $L_2 \ge 2D$ saves $\min(2D,L_1)/(2L_1) - D/L_2$ bits per message.

\section{How many bits is a domain patch?}\label{sec:patches}

A patch is itself a transmitted bitstring, so the counting arguments apply to the patch
alphabet. If a shared decompressor is steered to $K$ distinct domains, the assignment of
patches to domains is injective: two domains sharing a patch would leave the deployed
decoder in the same state, contradicting that each has its own optimal state.

\begin{theorem}[Some patch is expensive]\label{thm:expensive}
If $2^{t+1}\le K$ then some domain's patch exceeds $t$ bits; the longest patch is at least
$\log_2 K - 1$ bits.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Logarithmic warm-up delay]\label{thm:warmup}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:expensive} there is a domain $k$ such that, in the
two-state model with $D = |\mathrm{patch}(k)|$,
\[
V(\gen, c^{\,n}) = n(r+1) \qquad \text{for every } n \le t .
\]
For the first $t \approx \log_2 K$ messages the optimal adaptive protocol coincides exactly
with the generic never-patch protocol: the specialized decoder is worth precisely nothing.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Choose $k$ with $|\mathrm{patch}(k)| > t$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:expensive} and apply
Corollary~\ref{cor:short} with $D = |\mathrm{patch}(k)| \ge n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
For that same domain, any stream on which the adaptive protocol strictly beats the generic
one has length $> t$; and $\sum_{k=1}^{K} |\mathrm{patch}(k)| > t \approx \log_2 K$.
\end{theorem}

A shared decompressor advertised as serving many domains must pay, somewhere, for the
address space in which those domains are named. Reparametrizations of the patch --- adapter
rank, sparsity pattern, quantization --- move bits around but cannot beat the counting bound
without shrinking the number of reachable domains.

\section{Structural laws of the optimum}\label{sec:structure}

\begin{theorem}[Superadditivity]
$V_\delta(p,\mathbf{c}) + \min_{m} V_\delta(m,\mathbf{d}) \le V_\delta(p, \mathbf{c}\cdot\mathbf{d})$.
\end{theorem}

Splitting a stream never helps the optimum; the only advantage of global knowledge is the
choice of decoder state at the splice point. This is the min-plus counterpart of
submultiplicativity of matrix norms.

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity]
If $\delta \le \delta'$ entrywise then $V_\delta \le V_{\delta'}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Switching bound]
If $\delta$ satisfies the triangle inequality then
$V_\delta(s,\mathbf{c}) \le \delta(s,s') + V_\delta(s',\mathbf{c})$ for all $s,s'$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Triangle inequality is min-plus idempotence]
Let $(\delta\odot\delta)(i,j) = \min_k(\delta(i,k)+\delta(k,j))$ and assume
$\delta(m,m)=0$. Then $\delta\odot\delta = \delta$ if and only if $\delta$ satisfies the
triangle inequality, and in that case $V_{\delta\odot\delta} = V_\delta$: allowing the
encoder to compose two patches leaves the optimum unchanged.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\delta\odot\delta\le\delta$ always, by routing through $i$ itself. Equality at $(i,j)$ says
no intermediate $k$ beats the direct patch, which is the triangle inequality; conversely the
triangle inequality makes $\delta$ a lower bound for every routed cost.
\end{proof}

Since any reasonable patch format allows concatenation, the triangle inequality holds in
practice and multi-hop patch routing is a dead end.

\section{Algorithms}\label{sec:algorithms}

\paragraph{Scheduling dynamic program.} With $|M| = k$ states and $n$ messages the backward
recursion computes the optimum and an optimal schedule in $O(nk^2)$ time and $O(k)$ working
space (plus $O(nk)$ for argmin pointers). It is exactly a shortest path in the layered
digraph with $n+1$ layers of $k$ nodes and edge weight $\delta(m,m') + c_i(m')$; correctness
is Theorem~\ref{thm:bellman}.

\paragraph{Tropical matrix powering.} For coherent streams Theorem~\ref{thm:power} replaces
the $O(nk^2)$ sweep by min-plus exponentiation by squaring: $O(k^3\log n)$, essentially
independent of stream length.

\paragraph{Threshold (``commit for $D$'') scheduling.} Corollaries~\ref{cor:breakeven} and
\ref{cor:short} and the block law all express one rule: switch exactly when the coherent run
served is at least as long as the delta. The resulting linear-time greedy scheduler is
provably optimal on coherent and block-alternating streams; its status on arbitrary streams
is Conjecture~\ref{conj:c2}.

\paragraph{Diagnostics.} From measured residual advantage, patch length $D$ and empirical
coherence length $L$, the theory yields the break-even length ($n = D$), the achievable
excess rate ($\min(2D,L)/(2L)$), and the value of sorting --- all one-line computations that
should precede any engineering.

\section{Applications and discussion}\label{sec:applications}

\emph{Neural compression with adapters.} The shared decompressor is a pretrained sequence
model, $r$ the residual rate of the adapted model, $r+1$ that of the generic model in units
of the per-message advantage, and $D$ the serialized adapter size.
Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} gives the exact budget, Corollary~\ref{cor:breakeven} the exact
break-even, Theorem~\ref{thm:coh} the achievable rate on realistic multi-domain traffic.

\emph{Shared dictionaries.} Dictionary-based codecs instantiate the same model with $D$ the
dictionary size: the dictionary must be shorter than the coherent run it serves.

\emph{Federated and edge deployments.} Theorem~\ref{thm:warmup} quantifies the warm-up:
serving $K$ device profiles means some profile receives no benefit at all for its first
$\approx \log_2 K$ messages.

\emph{Database and log compression.} Column stores and log pipelines choose a codec per
block; Theorem~\ref{thm:coh} says a block must span at least $2D$ messages for a per-block
codec switch to pay.

\emph{Limitations.} Costs are integer-valued and the specialization advantage is normalized
to one bit; both are conveniences that permit sharpness and neither restricts the
qualitative conclusions. Residual rates are taken as given: the theory says nothing about
how good a model can be, only how to schedule between models of known quality. The state
set is finite; continuous adapter families would need a metric delta cost, and the counting
bounds would become covering-number bounds. Hypothesis (H2) encodes that specialization is
genuinely gated behind a patch; a decoder that could drift into a specialized state for free
would evade Theorem~\ref{thm:lower}, and correctly so.

\section{Future directions}\label{sec:future}

\begin{conjecture}[Amortized rate is a tropical eigenvalue]\label{conj:c1}
For a finite state set, a delta cost and a periodic stream of residual costs, the limit
$V(n)/n$ exists and equals the minimum cycle mean of the associated min-plus cost matrix ---
its tropical eigenvalue --- and the optimal protocol is eventually periodic with that cycle.
\end{conjecture}

Theorem~\ref{thm:power} already identifies the optimum with a tropical matrix power, so the
asymptotic bits per message is the growth rate of tropical powers, which for min-plus
matrices is the minimum cycle mean. Both special cases are proved: coherent streams give
cycle mean $r$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:asym}), block-alternating streams give
$r + \min(2D,L)/(2L)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:coh}). A general Karp-style theorem would subsume
both and give a computable design rule for adaptation schedules.

\begin{conjecture}[Hysteretic optimal schedules with threshold $D$]\label{conj:c2}
For every cost stream an optimal schedule exists that changes decoder state at message $i$
only if the state it enters is optimal for at least $D$ of the next messages; and this
``commit for $D$ messages'' rule is optimal within an additive $D$ bits.
\end{conjecture}

All sharp results so far --- $nr+\min(D,n)$, break-even exactly at $n=D$, and the block law
$\min(2D,L)$ --- express the same trade-off: a switch is worth it iff the coherent run it
serves is at least as long as the delta. Since the exact block optimum shows mixed policies
can beat both pure policies, a provable greedy/threshold rule with an additive-$D$ guarantee
is the natural next theorem, directly testable against the dynamic program.

\begin{conjecture}[The $\log K$ warm-up is unavoidable on average]\label{conj:c3}
If a shared decompressor serves $K$ domains with prefix-free patches, the \emph{average}
patch length is at least $\log_2 K - O(1)$, hence the average break-even stream length over
domains is $\Omega(\log K)$; no reparametrization can beat this without shrinking the number
of reachable domains.
\end{conjecture}

Theorem~\ref{thm:expensive} is a pigeonhole statement about the patch alphabet itself, and
Kraft's inequality upgrades a worst-case counting bound to an average-case one.

Beyond these: a continuous version in which adapters form a metric space and the delta cost
is a quantization cost, replacing counting bounds by covering numbers; a stochastic version
in which the domain evolves as a Markov chain and the coherence length is its expected
sojourn time; and an online version in which the sender does not know the future stream,
where the natural target is a competitive ratio against the offline optimum.

\section{Conclusion}

Compression with a shared, adaptable decompressor is a scheduling problem over decoder
states, and that problem is min-plus linear. Working in the tropical semiring turns
qualitative folklore into exact statements: the optimum for a coherent stream is exactly
$nr + \min(D,n)$ bits; the break-even is exactly at $n = D$; short streams gain exactly
nothing; the amortized rate on a block-alternating stream is exactly $r+\min(2D,L)/(2L)$;
and a decompressor serving $K$ domains has a domain with an $\Omega(\log K)$-bit patch and a
correspondingly delayed break-even. Against the pigeonhole floor of $ns$ bits the amortized
protocol is optimal to within the one-off delta, uniformly in stream length, with exact
lossless reconstruction. The single sentence to take away: \emph{the model delta amortizes
against the coherence length of the stream, not against its length.}

